WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.010
- Did you know that the spelling bee

00:00:03.010 --> 00:00:05.230
was invented in the United States?

00:00:05.230 --> 00:00:06.920
Yeah, that's not a flex.

00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:09.710
Spelling bees are kind of
pointless outside English,

00:00:09.710 --> 00:00:12.240
because most other languages
have spelling systems

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:14.360
that are regular and predictable,

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:16.690
but English spelling is such a mess

00:00:16.690 --> 00:00:18.810
that just being able to do it correctly

00:00:18.810 --> 00:00:20.120
can earn you a trophy.

00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:23.940
These two words rhyme,
but these two don't.

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:25.600
These two words rhyme.

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:27.060
These two don't.

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:29.970
These two rhyme, but these two don't.

00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:33.520
In fact, none of these words
rhyme with any of the others.

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:35.740
English is full of these contradictions,

00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:38.540
making it one of the
hardest languages to learn.

00:00:38.540 --> 00:00:40.900
Almost every spelling rule

00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:43.340
comes with a long list of exceptions.

00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:45.840
I before E except after C.

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:47.961
Yeah, well, what about these?

00:00:47.961 --> 00:00:49.560
Weird, huh?

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:51.030
An E at the end of the word

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:53.050
supposedly makes the vowel long,

00:00:53.050 --> 00:00:55.960
like in home and hive and hate.

00:00:55.960 --> 00:00:59.130
Well, I'd love for someone to give me

00:00:59.130 --> 00:01:01.060
an explanation for these.

00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:03.390
Many letters can have multiple sounds,

00:01:03.390 --> 00:01:06.720
and others seem to serve
no purpose whatsoever.

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:10.960
A K in knife, an H in
ghost, an S in island?

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:13.430
In fact, almost every
letter of the alphabet

00:01:13.430 --> 00:01:15.080
is silent somewhere.

00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:16.790
Yeah, I got the receipts.

00:01:16.790 --> 00:01:18.490
Some have accused English spelling

00:01:18.490 --> 00:01:22.680
of being irrational and
arbitrary, but, actually,

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:26.060
there are good reasons why
our spellings are so strange.

00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:29.400
Well, some good reasons,
some snobby reasons,

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:32.255
and some that are downright dum-buh.

00:01:32.255 --> 00:01:35.833
I'm Dr. Erica Brozovsky
and this is "Other Words".

00:01:35.833 --> 00:01:38.500
(curious music)

00:01:43.095 --> 00:01:44.428
- "Other Words".

00:01:46.390 --> 00:01:49.500
- English's wacky spelling
goes all the way back

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:51.130
to the origin of the alphabet.

00:01:51.130 --> 00:01:54.810
Old English was a Germanic
language spoken by Anglo Saxons

00:01:54.810 --> 00:01:57.700
who used a runic alphabet
known as a Futhorc.

00:01:57.700 --> 00:01:59.940
As Latin missionaries
Christianized England

00:01:59.940 --> 00:02:02.570
during the seventh century,
they replaced the Futhorc

00:02:02.570 --> 00:02:04.000
with the Latin alphabet.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:06.300
The thing is the Latin alphabet

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:08.700
was not designed for a Germanic tongue.

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:10.610
There were sounds that Anglo Saxons used

00:02:10.610 --> 00:02:13.180
that had no equivalent in Latin.

00:02:13.180 --> 00:02:15.300
This required some improvisations.

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:18.150
Some letters had to do
double duty, like C,

00:02:18.150 --> 00:02:20.310
which stood for [k], as in macian

00:02:21.160 --> 00:02:23.400
and [ch], as in circe,

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:25.610
and a couple runic
letters were kept around,

00:02:25.610 --> 00:02:28.450
like wynn, which signified the [w-] sound

00:02:28.450 --> 00:02:31.170
and thorn, which made the [th] sound.

00:02:31.170 --> 00:02:34.610
As you can see, the English
alphabet had barely begun

00:02:34.610 --> 00:02:37.400
and it was already
getting a bit confusing.

00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:39.840
But plenty of other non-Latin languages

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:42.890
use the same alphabet, like
German, Dutch, and Czech,

00:02:42.890 --> 00:02:45.900
and their spellings,
while not exactly easy,

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:47.530
are at least consistent.

00:02:47.530 --> 00:02:51.189
So how did English spelling
get so uniquely screwed up?

00:02:51.189 --> 00:02:53.050
(cheerful music)

00:02:53.050 --> 00:02:57.430
In 1066, William the Conqueror
invaded England from Normandy

00:02:57.430 --> 00:03:00.150
and installed French
speaking nobles to rule it.

00:03:00.150 --> 00:03:03.280
Not only did they bring a ton
of French words with them,

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:05.310
but the Norman scribes
changed the spelling

00:03:05.310 --> 00:03:06.710
of a lot of English words

00:03:06.710 --> 00:03:08.830
to look more familiar to French readers.

00:03:08.830 --> 00:03:10.270
One of the first things they did

00:03:10.270 --> 00:03:13.130
was get rid of those pesky runic letters.

00:03:13.130 --> 00:03:16.980
They replaced the thorn with
T-H and the wynn with two Us,

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:19.970
which eventually became,
what do you know, W.

00:03:19.970 --> 00:03:23.210
In French, the ooh sound was spelled O-U,

00:03:23.210 --> 00:03:25.420
as in crouton and rouge,

00:03:25.420 --> 00:03:30.020
so old English words like
hus and hund had Os added.

00:03:30.020 --> 00:03:33.520
The French used G-H to
signify the [kh] sound,

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:37.630
so the old English words
niht and miht had Gs added.

00:03:37.630 --> 00:03:40.070
By the time the Normans
were done tinkering with it,

00:03:40.070 --> 00:03:42.960
the Middle English spelling
system was already a jumble

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:45.920
of Germanic, Latin,
and French conventions,

00:03:45.920 --> 00:03:48.590
but two big events in linguistic history

00:03:48.590 --> 00:03:51.563
were about to make
things much, much worse.

00:03:53.550 --> 00:03:55.790
Between 1400 and 1700,

00:03:55.790 --> 00:03:58.980
the pronunciations of many
English vowels shifted.

00:03:58.980 --> 00:04:01.450
No one knows exactly why this happened,

00:04:01.450 --> 00:04:02.760
but it's essentially what created

00:04:02.760 --> 00:04:05.160
the modern English sounds we use today.

00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:07.740
For instance, the [oo] sound became [ow],

00:04:07.740 --> 00:04:11.630
so hoose and hoond became house and hound.

00:04:11.630 --> 00:04:15.166
[Ah] became [eh], so nah-meh became name-eh.

00:04:15.166 --> 00:04:19.250
[Ee] became [ai], so lee-keh became like-eh.

00:04:19.250 --> 00:04:22.460
A lot of words lost their
second syllables altogether,

00:04:22.460 --> 00:04:26.380
so name-eh and like-eh
became name and like.

00:04:26.380 --> 00:04:29.470
We still commemorate that second
syllable with the silent E.

00:04:29.470 --> 00:04:32.210
Other consonant sounds faded away as well.

00:04:32.210 --> 00:04:34.010
Dum-buh became dumb.

00:04:34.010 --> 00:04:37.890
Ni-kht and li-kht became night and light.

00:04:37.890 --> 00:04:41.460
Keh-nife and keh-nikht
became knife and night.

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:42.950
You might be thinking,

00:04:42.950 --> 00:04:45.170
if you're not gonna
pronounce the sounds anymore,

00:04:45.170 --> 00:04:47.660
why not get rid of the extra letters?

00:04:47.660 --> 00:04:49.360
Well, some scholars at the time

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:51.160
did want to update the spelling system

00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:53.080
to match the new pronunciations,

00:04:53.080 --> 00:04:55.380
but something was happening concurrently

00:04:55.380 --> 00:04:59.052
that would lock in those
outdated spellings permanently.

00:04:59.052 --> 00:05:01.230
(playful music)

00:05:01.230 --> 00:05:03.670
Around 1440, Johannes Gutenberg

00:05:03.670 --> 00:05:05.790
invented the printing press in Germany,

00:05:05.790 --> 00:05:09.350
and within 100 years, it had
spread all across Europe.

00:05:09.350 --> 00:05:11.250
In England, this meant that spellings

00:05:11.250 --> 00:05:13.640
had to become standardized
right at the time

00:05:13.640 --> 00:05:15.460
that pronunciations were shifting.

00:05:15.460 --> 00:05:18.290
Moreover, the people who
did the standardizing

00:05:18.290 --> 00:05:20.500
didn't always have the best reasons.

00:05:20.500 --> 00:05:22.560
Dutch type setters changed some spellings

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:24.350
to match their Flemish conventions,

00:05:24.350 --> 00:05:27.570
like adding an H to ghost and ghastly.

00:05:27.570 --> 00:05:29.930
And since dumb ended with a B,

00:05:29.930 --> 00:05:33.920
some printers mistakenly added
Bs to limb, crumb, and numb,

00:05:33.920 --> 00:05:35.760
an error known as hypercorrection.

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:38.930
There was also a lot of snobbery going on.

00:05:38.930 --> 00:05:41.110
By now, the Renaissance was in full swing,

00:05:41.110 --> 00:05:43.840
and scholars were importing
tons of fancy words

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:47.840
from Greek, Latin, and French
with their fancy spellings.

00:05:47.840 --> 00:05:51.320
These were derogatorily
known as "inkhorn words,"

00:05:51.320 --> 00:05:54.010
named for the ink pots
that nerds and know-it-alls

00:05:54.010 --> 00:05:55.440
kept on their desks.

00:05:55.440 --> 00:05:57.310
As if that wasn't bad enough,

00:05:57.310 --> 00:06:00.610
the Renaissance fanboys also
wanted to change the spellings

00:06:00.610 --> 00:06:02.570
of existing English words

00:06:02.570 --> 00:06:05.260
to pay tribute to their
etymological roots.

00:06:05.260 --> 00:06:06.910
Words of Greek dissent were signified

00:06:06.910 --> 00:06:10.980
by adding an H after the C,
as in chronicle and chaos,

00:06:10.980 --> 00:06:12.960
and substituting P-H for F,

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:15.510
as in physics, phlegm, and pharmacy.

00:06:15.510 --> 00:06:19.370
And words of Latin origin,
like det, dout, sine,

00:06:19.370 --> 00:06:22.990
receit, indite, and ile had letters added,

00:06:22.990 --> 00:06:26.360
not to be pronounced, but
just as a reminder to readers

00:06:26.360 --> 00:06:29.220
where those words originally came from.

00:06:29.220 --> 00:06:31.570
In another instance of hypercorrection,

00:06:31.570 --> 00:06:34.660
they even added an S to the word island,

00:06:34.660 --> 00:06:38.070
mistakenly believing it was
related to the word isle.

00:06:38.070 --> 00:06:41.160
In fact, the two words have
totally different origins.

00:06:41.160 --> 00:06:43.040
Classic case of over-nerding.

00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:44.960
The end of the great vowel shift

00:06:44.960 --> 00:06:47.530
represents the beginning
of modern English,

00:06:47.530 --> 00:06:49.390
but thanks to the printing press,

00:06:49.390 --> 00:06:50.920
it was stuck with a spelling system

00:06:50.920 --> 00:06:52.690
that was based on Germanic roots

00:06:52.690 --> 00:06:55.070
with a Latin script using French spellings

00:06:55.070 --> 00:06:58.360
to describe Middle English pronunciations.

00:06:58.360 --> 00:07:01.400
Some have attempted to
modernize English spelling,

00:07:01.400 --> 00:07:04.710
most notably Noah Webster,
who in the early 1800s

00:07:04.710 --> 00:07:07.810
suggested a bunch of
phonetically corrected changes.

00:07:07.810 --> 00:07:10.443
Some stuck, others did not.

00:07:11.610 --> 00:07:13.470
This list kind of reminds me

00:07:13.470 --> 00:07:15.550
of the recent trend in phonetic spelling

00:07:15.550 --> 00:07:18.400
popularized by texting and social media.

00:07:18.400 --> 00:07:20.910
Though it's typically employed
for humor and brevity,

00:07:20.910 --> 00:07:23.450
it does speak to an underlying desire

00:07:23.450 --> 00:07:24.500
for our written language

00:07:24.500 --> 00:07:27.150
to more closely match our spoken one.

00:07:27.150 --> 00:07:29.760
While it may seem
far-fetched that dictionaries

00:07:29.760 --> 00:07:31.900
would ever make these things official,

00:07:31.900 --> 00:07:33.400
stranger things have happened.

00:07:33.400 --> 00:07:36.040
Until then, we can at least take comfort

00:07:36.040 --> 00:07:38.880
knowing that our spelling
quirks are evidence of language

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:42.950
that is uniquely flexible,
absorbent, and multicultural.

00:07:42.950 --> 00:07:45.120
And that we live in
the age of spell-check.

00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:48.170
I take great comfort in spell-check.

00:07:48.170 --> 00:07:49.683
Ni-ght and li-ght.

00:07:50.670 --> 00:07:54.587
More phlegm, ni-ght,
ni-ght, ni-ght and li-ght.

00:07:55.762 --> 00:07:57.429
Ni-ght and, I can't.

00:07:58.267 --> 00:08:00.000
Ni-ght, keh-ni-ght.

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:02.023
I never have to say ni-ght again.

